  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 233 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Warm and humid conditions are expected to persist \nthroughout the day today with plenty of moisture streaming into the \nregion as we remain in between a Bermuda High and an upper low \ncentered over Hudson Bay. Shower activity this morning continues \nacross the region, slowly moving northeastward. Heavy rainfall has \nbeen the main concern with these storms, as several locations have \nreceived over 2 inches of rainfall within rather short periods of \ntime, for example Essex County, NY where Flash Flood Warnings were \nissued overnight. Precipitation is expected to wane towards the \nmorning, before additional rounds of convection develop later in the \nday. Model soundings show CAPE values of 1500 J/kg or so, with \nrelatively low wind shear, with the environment favorable for pulse \nthunderstorms. Any thunderstorms that develop will likely be garden-\nvariety, but a stronger storm can't be entirely ruled out. The main \nconcern this afternoon will once again be heavy rainfall and any \npossible hydrological/flooding concerns, especially in locations \nhave already received decent rainfall over the last few days. WPC \ncontinues to have a Marginal ERO across the region for tomorrow. \nAnother round of showers is expected to move through the region \ntonight into Sunday, although with little instability these showers \nshould be rather unimpactful. A brief break in the unsettled pattern \nis expected to develop through the day Sunday as a narrow axis of \nhigh pressure moves over the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A brief period of high pressure will bring relatively \ntranquil weather to start the week, but unsettled weather is \nexpected to return Monday evening ahead of an approaching cold \nfront. The threat of showers and thunderstorms will ramp up \nthroughout the evening, with a bulk of the precipitation expected \novernight into early Tuesday.Stronger storms currently look unlikely \nwith only modest instability and shear, but we'll have to monitor \nany trends in the data if they deviate from the current thinking. \nTemperatures on Monday will be a few degrees cooler, with high \ntemperatures in the mid 70s to mid 80s across the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: The long wave pattern is projected to remain zonal \nacross the Northeast through next week with unblocked flow \ndownstream and largely the result of a massive Omega High centered \nover the Western US and Canada. The result will be for quick moving \nsystems tracking through the long wave pattern bringing periodic \nshowers and thunderstorms. The nature of this pattern will promote \nweaker systems and lean towards a slow cooling trend with \ntemperatures favored to dip below seasonal averages by the end of \nthe work week as the Bermuda High retreats southeastward. As such, \nhighs in the 80s Tuesday will decrease into the mid/upper 70s by \nFriday.",
